1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,950
Every technique in this course names its cost, and this is the honest edge of what

2
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:09,661
agents earn you: hours back on the work around a decision, never the decision itself

3
00:00:09,661 --> 00:00:11,176
where it genuinely matters.

4
00:00:11,176 --> 00:00:15,495
Priya's final sign-off on binding a commercial policy is exactly that kind of

5
00:00:15,495 --> 00:00:18,748
decision, and she has never once considered automating it.

6
00:00:18,748 --> 00:00:23,123
Notice where she says it: out loud, to her director, as part of the pitch, not

7
00:00:23,123 --> 00:00:24,301
buried in a footnote.

8
00:00:24,301 --> 00:00:27,386
That placement is doing persuasion work worth studying.

9
00:00:27,386 --> 00:00:31,930
A person who names what they will not automate is instantly more believable about

10
00:00:31,930 --> 00:00:36,585
everything they will, because they have demonstrated the one thing a manager cannot

11
00:00:36,585 --> 00:00:39,054
verify from a slide: judgement about limits.

12
00:00:39,054 --> 00:00:41,690
And in her trade the example is not rhetorical.

13
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:46,289
Binding is the moment cover legally exists; an error at that step commits the firm

14
00:00:46,289 --> 00:00:46,682
itself.

15
00:00:46,682 --> 00:00:51,338
Some doors should have a human hand on them because of what is behind the door, not

16
00:00:51,338 --> 00:00:55,769
because of any doubt about the agent, and naming those doors unprompted is what

17
00:00:55,769 --> 00:01:00,425
separates an operator from an enthusiast in the eyes of everyone whose sign-off you

18
00:01:00,425 --> 00:01:01,266
will ever need.

19
00:01:01,816 --> 00:01:04,052
Notice this is a short list, on purpose.

20
00:01:04,052 --> 00:01:08,467
The temptation after four successful builds is to reach for a longer one out of

21
00:01:08,467 --> 00:01:08,914
caution.

22
00:01:08,914 --> 00:01:13,441
A list so long it excludes half your job is not a safety measure, it is an excuse

23
00:01:13,441 --> 00:01:15,957
not to get the hours back you actually could.

24
00:01:15,957 --> 00:01:18,807
Why does the middle category deserve a second look?

25
00:01:18,807 --> 00:01:20,428
Because it is the subtle one.

26
00:01:20,428 --> 00:01:24,676
An unusual claim is not hard because the stakes are high, it is hard because

27
00:01:24,676 --> 00:01:29,315
"legitimate" cannot be specified in advance: each one turns on context nobody could

28
00:01:29,315 --> 00:01:30,768
enumerate into a briefing.

29
00:01:30,768 --> 00:01:35,351
There is a handy tell for this category, and you met it in Section 1.4: if you sit

30
00:01:35,351 --> 00:01:39,990
down to write question five of the job description, what correct means in this job,

31
00:01:39,990 --> 00:01:43,567
and find you genuinely cannot, you are not failing at paperwork.

32
00:01:43,567 --> 00:01:48,206
You are holding a task that belongs on this list, and the template just told you so

33
00:01:48,206 --> 00:01:50,721
before any build wasted a weekend proving it.

34
00:01:51,271 --> 00:01:55,512
Run every agent you have ever built, or ever plan to, through that question.

35
00:01:55,512 --> 00:02:00,088
It is the same test, asked one more way, that Section 1's blast radius and Section

36
00:02:00,088 --> 00:02:02,767
6's draft-only design were both built to answer.

37
00:02:02,767 --> 00:02:05,334
Notice what the test is not about: importance.

38
00:02:05,334 --> 00:02:10,021
That is where most never-automate lists go wrong, banning tasks for being important,

39
00:02:10,021 --> 00:02:12,421
which is really banning them for mattering.

40
00:02:12,421 --> 00:02:16,997
Plenty of important work automates beautifully because its failures are catchable:

41
00:02:16,997 --> 00:02:21,461
the weekly report matters to Marcus, and a wrong draft costs a correction, so it

42
00:02:21,461 --> 00:02:22,019
automates.

43
00:02:22,019 --> 00:02:26,707
And occasionally a trivial-seeming task belongs on the list because its failure path

44
00:02:26,707 --> 00:02:27,376
has no undo.

45
00:02:27,376 --> 00:02:32,008
The sorting question is always about the failure's shape, never the task's dignity,

46
00:02:32,008 --> 00:02:35,468
and once you see that, your list writes itself in ten minutes.

47
00:02:36,018 --> 00:02:40,517
Treat the list as a living document with a high bar for edits in both directions.

48
00:02:40,517 --> 00:02:45,016
Removing an item should take a genuine change in the world, a real undo appearing

49
00:02:45,016 --> 00:02:46,071
where none existed.

50
00:02:46,071 --> 00:02:50,182
Adding one should take a named irreversible step, not a bad week's nerves.

51
00:02:50,182 --> 00:02:54,625
And keep it visible, because its audience was never only you: the next section's

52
00:02:54,625 --> 00:02:59,291
whole pitch stands on Priya being the kind of operator who can show her director, on

53
00:02:59,291 --> 00:03:02,512
one page, both what she automated and what she refused to.

54
00:03:02,512 --> 00:03:06,845
With the costs counted and the limits named, next lecture closes this section.

55
00:03:06,845 --> 00:03:11,510
Section 10 is where all of this becomes something you can actually bring to your own

56
00:03:11,510 --> 00:03:11,788
team.
